tests/boot — the machine booting real DOS from disk images

The images live in disks/freedos/ (git-ignored; FreeDOS 1.3 is GPL):

  disks/freedos/floppy/144m/   FD13-FloppyEdition.zip, 1.44M disks:
                               x86BOOT.img, x86DSK01..05.img
  disks/freedos/lite/          FD13-LiteUSB.zip (not used yet)
  disks/freedos/c.img          128 MB hard disk, FreeDOS installed (386), WP51
  disks/freedos/c286.img       the same installed on a 286

from https://www.ibiblio.org/pub/micro/pc-stuff/freedos/files/distributions/1.3/official/

c.img was made by the installer itself, on a blank image:

  dd if=/dev/zero of=disks/freedos/c.img bs=1m count=128
  F=disks/freedos/floppy/144m
  tools/expect.py -v -t 900 tests/boot/fdinstall.exp -- ./dos-monster -m 386 -W -T 920 \
      -fda $F/x86BOOT.img:$F/x86DSK01.img:$F/x86DSK02.img:$F/x86DSK03.img:$F/x86DSK04.img:$F/x86DSK05.img \
      -hda disks/freedos/c.img -boot a

It partitions (FDISK through INT 13h), reboots (the reset vector), formats,
writes the system, extracts all five diskettes (1544 files) and finishes;
fdinstall.exp answers N to the closing reboot. c286.img is the same with
-m 286 (the 8086 kernel), booted on a 286 by run.sh.

The first installs died after the extraction — a jump into data inside
XCOPY on a 286, a runaway on a 386. That was the JIT: INT n and CALL FAR
[mem] held the target CS:IP in scratch registers across the frame's
pushes, and a push onto memory that had held translated code (SLICEREX's,
freed, reused as the next program's stack) fired the SMC check, whose
thunk did not save them. tests/dos/smcfar.com is the reduction.

Configuration changed on c.img (mtools, CR LF kept): FDCONFIG.SYS gets
MENU 6 — HIMEMX with DOS=HIGH, no JEMM (JEMMEX/JEMM386 need V86 mode,
which the machine does not have yet) — as the default, with a 1-second
menu (MENUDEFAULT=6,1); FDAUTO.BAT skips the CD-ROM probe for every
entry (there is no IDE controller, and UDVD2 waits out its timeouts on
four status ports — 8 of the 14 s it took JEMMEX to reach WP). Boot to C:\> is ~3 s; MEM shows 16,448K XMS and "FreeDOS
is resident in the high memory area".

With the V86/paging campaign (milestones 1-2, the interpreter) the stock
menu entries work too: 2 (JEMMEX) runs DOS in virtual-8086 mode with
paging — UMBs, COMMAND and FDAPM loaded high, 629K conventional free — and
WordPerfect runs under it; 3 (JEMM386) adds 16M of EMS. V86 code and the
memory manager's flat ring 0 are translated (campaign milestones 4-5);
boot to WP's editing screen under JEMMEX takes ~6 s. The default stays
entry 6, the plainer machine.

WordPerfect was added with mtools:

  mcopy -s -i disks/freedos/c.img@@32256 disks/WP51 ::/

(the partition starts at sector 63). run.sh boots scratch copies, so the
image never changes under a test.

MS-DOS 6.22 (disks/msdos622/, git-ignored: the user's own diskettes,
msdos622-1..3.img, and c.img installed from them by msinstall.sh)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

  tests/boot/msinstall.sh            # ~25 s, blank disk to C:\> with C:\DOS

Setup (the upgrade edition) won't touch a blank disk, so the script exits
it, partitions with FDISK (it reboots through the reset vector), formats
with FORMAT C: /S, and lets Setup finish over the fresh system ("Continue
Setup and replace"). Diskette changes are expect.py's ESC + key. run.sh
boots it as Setup left it (HIMEM, DOS=HIGH), then with EMM386.EXE RAM,
DOS=HIGH,UMB and LH SMARTDRV (V86 mode, paging: 16M EMS, UMBs), then
WordPerfect under that.

What it took, beyond FreeDOS:
- IO.SYS times the keyboard controller's ACK by counting port 61h's
  refresh toggle (bit 4, every 15 us), and wants the FAh ACK itself.
- INT 16h AH=00h can't wait in the host once INT 9 is hooked (IO.SYS,
  KEYB): it returns unfinished with IF set and the INT reruns.
- IO.SYS runs a stack on its own code: a CALL FAR's push into the running
  block must not leave the block mid-instruction (the SMC check of a block
  ender names no running block).
- EMM386 looks for its page frame in open bus: C0000-EFFFF read FFh and
  ignore writes (X86_BM_EMPTY), and a not-present gate that also has a
  bad type is #GP, not #NP (QEMU as the oracle: -d int).
- The BIOS's INT 8 chains INT 1Ch through a real stub (INT 1Ch; IRET at
  F100:0000), so a V86 monitor reflects it like any other interrupt.
- POST sets the tick count from the RTC, so TIME is right at boot.

DOOM (shareware, disks/doom) under MS-DOS: run.sh copies it onto the
EMM386 image and onto a plain HIMEM one. Under EMM386, DOS/4GW enters
protected mode through VCPI and builds its own page tables; without it,
it switches itself over XMS memory. Both needed:
- a real master 8259: ICW1-4 (the vector base), OCW3 (port 20h reads the
  IRR, or the ISR after 0Bh) and a mask that is honoured. DOS/4GW under
  VCPI tells IRQ 0 from a double fault, both vector 8, by the ISR; a port
  20h that read 0 made the first tick "exception 08h (double fault)".
- the JIT fetching a paged code page mapped BELOW its linear address
  (DOOM at 498000 on physical 3ED000): the decode added the 32-bit
  physical-minus-linear delta to the host pointer unwrapped, 4 GB past
  guest memory. pgtest's T0E runs code on such a page.

Windows 3.11 (disks/win311/, git-ignored: the user's OEM 3.5" diskettes
in oem/DISK1-6.IMG, and c.img installed from them)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

  tests/boot/wininstall.sh           # ~3 min: MS-DOS disk + Setup, end to end

The diskettes are flattened into C:\WININST (no CD-ROM on the machine;
Setup finds each disk's files by the DISKn markers). Setup's second half
is Windows itself in standard mode, drawn in VGA mode 12h, so the script
drives it blind with expect.py's "send" and "delay"; "shot FILE" saves
the picture (X86_SCREEN_PNG, rewritten once a second) for looking at.
run.sh starts "win /s" and checks Program Manager's title bar pixel
(tools/pngpix.py). Plain "win" (386 enhanced mode) says "Video
initialization failed" — the next campaign.

What standard mode needed:
- an inward CALL through a gate or an interrupt reading its new stack
  from a 286 TSS (SP0 at 2, SS0 at 4, four bytes a ring). DOSX has one;
  the 386 layout read a null SS, and the #SS handler's own CALL faulted
  on it forever. A null SS there is #TS now.
- VGA modes 0Dh/0Eh/10h/12h: planar from the mode set, drawn through
  the attribute palette (pc_vga_frame reports its size; the window and
  -G follow).
- translated reads of the VGA window while the planar VGA answers them:
  blocks translated then send such reads (and MOVS/LODS/CMPS/SCAS) to
  the interpreter, which loads the latches; cpu->dev_hook retranslates
  when the planar form comes or goes. Without it Windows' fills and
  copies through the latches drew nothing.
- scripted keys for a program that reads IRQ 1 but calls INT 16h now
  and then: fed after 250 ms without BIOS polling, not only after total
  silence (Windows took the first key and no more).
